# Farm building studies from the park's files (typescripts; OCR noise): Brien farm study, Leister barn Historic Structure Report, McClean house and barn report (1968), Slyder farm HSR.
# Quotes are typed in clean spelling; every OCR letter error is shown with {{saved|fixed}} so that the text-layer reading and the correction are both on the record.
@ NPS-BRIEN-FARM
%basis tax records, the 1860 census, Brien's damage claims, soldiers' accounts and Brady photographs, as named in the study
6 | buildings | Brien farm: 12 acres, two houses on the Emmitsburg Road property | bld-brian-farm | pre-1863 (the purchase year is garbled in the text layer) | stated | Abraham Brien bought 12 acres south of Gettysburg; two houses stood on the Emmitsburg Road property and the family lived in the one farthest from the road | =Brien purchased 12 acres of land in Cumberland Township south of Gettysburg from James A. Thompson. The property, {{dying|lying}} at the southwest foot of Cemetery Hill, appears to have been divided, part of it fronting the Emmitsburg Road and part the Taneytown Road. Two houses stood on the Emmitsburg Road property, and Brien moved his family into the one farthest from the road.
7 | fields | Brien farm in July 1863: wheat, barley and hay, meadows and orchards | bld-brian-farm | 1863-07 | stated | In July 1863 Brien was farming wheat, barley and hay and had land in meadows and orchards | =In July, 1863, he was {{f a m i n g|farming}} wheat, barley, and hay and had land in meadows and orchards.
8 | fields | 111th New York in front of the Brien house: fields of wheat and a grove behind the house | bld-brian-farm | 1863-07-03 | stated | General MacDougall's 1891 dedication address placed the 111th New York in front of the Brien house, with fields of wheat to their front and a grove of trees behind the house where they bivouacked | =At the dedication of the 111th New York Monument, June 26, 1891, General Clinton MacDougall did describe his unit’s location in front of the Brien House, the fields of wheat to their foreground, and the grove of trees behind the house in which they bivouacked.
9 | orchards | Brien orchard at the rear of the house | bld-brian-farm | 1863-07-03 | stated | Captain David Shields wrote that he led the men of the 111th New York from the Brien orchard at the rear of the house into position when the cannonade began on July 3, 1863 | =Shields also states that he led the men of the 111th New York from the Brien orchard at the rear of the house into position when the cannonade started on July 3, 1863. | #s2
9 | buildings | Brady photographs show the east and south of the Brien house, the bake oven and the outline of the barn | bld-brian-farm | 1863-07 | hedged | Two photographs thought to be by Brady or his staff shortly after the battle show the east side and most of the south front of the house, the bake oven, an outline of the barn and the Briens' second house on the Emmitsburg Road | =One photograph was taken east of the Brien house looking west and clearly shows the east side of the house, most of its southern front, the bake oven, an outline of the {{b a m|barn}}, and a glimpse of the Brien’s second house on the {{Emnitsburg|Emmitsburg}} Road. | #s5

@ NPS-HSR-LEISTER-BARN
%basis the Gardner and other photographs of the barn and the park's research, as named in the report
9 | hospitals | Leister barn: signal station and temporary hospital; Leister house Meade's headquarters | bld-leister-house,H-16 | 1863-07 | stated | The report says the Leister barn was used as a signal station and temporary hospital during the battle and the adjacent Leister house served as Meade's headquarters for part of the battle | =Hie building was used as a signal station and temporary hospital during the Battle of Gettysburg and the adjacent Leister House was used as Meade’s {{Headquarter'sfor|Headquarters for}} part of the Battle.
9 | buildings | Leister barn to be restored to its appearance just before the battle in July 1863 | bld-leister-house | 1863-07 | stated | The report proposes restoring the exterior of the Leister barn to its appearance just before the battle in July 1863 | =It is {{preposed|proposed}} that the exterior of the barn be restored to its appearance just before the Battle in July, 1863.
13 | damage | Leister farm after the battle: dead horses in the yard, crops trampled | bld-leister-house | 1863-07 | stated | The report says dead horses littered the yard of the Leister farm and the crops were trampled | =Dead horses littered the yard, the crops were trampled
25 | fences | Fencing beside the Leister barn: post and rail east of the shed meeting a north-south wall and rider fence | bld-leister-house,FEN-leister,P-13 | 1863-07 (the pattern appears to have changed) | hedged | The report says the fencing pattern adjacent to the barn appears to have changed: two units of post and rail fence extend outward (east) from the SE corner of the shed to meet a north/south wall and rider fence, and together they enclosed an eastern barnyard | =The fencing pattern adjacent to the barn appears to have changed: extend outward two units of post and rail {{not|}} fence (east) from the SE corner of the shed to meet a north/south wall and rider fence. Together, they enclosed an eastern barnyard.

@ NPS-MCCLEAN-HOUSE-BARN
%basis Moses McClean's damage claim (No. 3248) as transcribed in the report
23 | buildings | McClean house in 1863: narrow, three rooms on each floor | - | 1863-07 | stated | The house in existence in 1863 (the McClean house studied in this report) was a narrow structure with three rooms on each floor | =The house in existence in 1863 was a narrow structure with 3 rooms on each floor.
26 | damage | Moses McClean claim: oats, wheat, hay, grass, straw, stone fence and board fence, corn | - | 1863-07 | stated | The transcription of McClean's claim lists 8 acres of oats (one third lost, 50 bushels), 13 acres of wheat (90 bushels lost), a ton of hay, 27 acres of grass (22 tons), 50 bundles of long straw, injury to stone fence with 4,546 rails and 35 posts and 10 panels of board fence lost, and 3 and 7 1/2 acres of corn driven over | =8 Acres Oats-1/3 loss 50 bushels 13 " Wheat 90 bushels lost 1 ton hay Injury done to grass 27 acres amounting to 22 tons $12.00 50 bundles long straw Injury to stone fence 4,546 rail lost 35 posts 10 Pannel board fence Damages by driving over farm 3 acres corn $10. per acre 7 1/2 acres corn $10. per acre

@ NPS-HSR-SLYDER
%basis the report's account (OR and regimental sources)
9 | fences | Stone wall near the Slyder house held by Company E and H, 2nd US Sharpshooters, July 2 | bld-slyder-farm | 1863-07-02 | stated | Early in the afternoon of July 2 advance Union positions lay near the Slyder buildings, Companies E and H of the 2nd US Sharpshooters being stationed at the stone wall near the house and Company D to the west along Slyder Lane | =advance Union positions were located in the vicinity of the Slyder buildings, Companies E and H, 2d Regiment, U. S. Sharpshooters, being stationed at the stone wall near the house, and Company D of the same organization to the west along Slyder Lane.
